13.4  Links, inodes and "disk full"

TL;DR
  - An inode is a file's record: owner, rights, size, where the data is. The
    name is stored in the folder, not in the inode.
  - A hard link is a second name for the same inode. A symbolic link is a small
    file that holds a path.
  - A filesystem can run out of inodes before space: "No space left on device"
    while df -h still shows room.

      df -i                       # inode use per filesystem
      ls -li                      # inode number of each file
      ln -s /data/photos photos   # a symbolic link named photos

On your machine
  nvme0n1         476.9 GiB  SSD · Samsung SSD 980 PRO 500GB
    nvme0n1p1     512.0 MiB  vfat   /boot/efi   256.0 MiB free (50%)
    nvme0n1p2     476.4 GiB  btrfs  / (+2)      120.0 GiB free (30%)
    nvme0n1p10      1.0 MiB
  sda              30.0 GiB  rotational · removable · USB DISK 3.0
    (whole disk)             exfat  /media/usb
  zram0             8.0 GiB  SSD · swap
  2 loop or empty devices not shown

Pitfalls
  - rm removes a name. The space comes back when the last name is gone and no
    program has the file open: list deleted but open files with sudo lsof +L1.
  - Millions of tiny files (caches, node_modules, mail) eat inodes. On ext4
    their number is fixed when the filesystem is created.
  - btrfs has no fixed inode count (df -i shows 0), and XFS creates inodes as
    needed: inode exhaustion is mostly an ext4 problem.
  - A hard link can't cross filesystems or point to a folder. A symbolic link
    can, but it breaks when its target moves.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 13.4 --why

